{"id":"R0014","key":"evaluation","s":"OBSERVED","f":"Indoor evaluation uses 24 UAV airborne computers (20 Jetson Nano, 2 Jetson Xavier TX2, 2 Jetson Xavier NX) connected via WiFi, whereas outdoor evaluation uses 5 real-world UAVs (4 Jetson Nano, 1 Jetson NX) communicating via Ad hoc modules.","l":[[10,"Fig. 12","24 UAV airborne computers through Wi-Fi connection"],[10,"Fig. 12","5 real-world UAVs through Ad-hoc connection"],[10,"VIII.A","communicate with each other through a WiFi network"],[11,"VIII.A","transmit data through the Ad hoc modules"]],"g":null}
{"id":"R0058","key":"architecture","s":"OBSERVED","f":"The architecture comprises autonomous UAVs and edge servers deployed at ground base stations; a distributed auction-based mechanism coordinates task allocation, with UAVs acting as buyers initiating inference tasks and edge servers acting as sellers offering computing resources.","l":[[5,"3.1","offloaded to edge servers within communication range"],[9,"4","propose a distributed auction-based mechanism that enables"],[9,"4","UAVs act as buyers that initiate inference tasks"],[9,"4","edge servers act as sellers that offer"]],"g":null}
{"id":"R0058","key":"mobility_continuity","s":"OBSERVED","f":"Described continuity (stages 1 and 5): UAVs estimate expected connection durations with candidate servers before disconnection, and upon failure from disconnection or limited resources, retry in the next round with a higher bid or buffer/execute locally in a simplified form if no server is available. Evaluated scope: simulations in §6.3 measured overall task success rate under varying UAV speeds, without isolating the retry or local-fallback mechanism.","l":[[9,"4.1","identifies the edge servers within communication range"],[10,"4.1","tasks can be completed before disconnection"],[10,"4.1","next round with a higher bid to reflect"],[10,"4.1","buffered or executed locally in a simplified form"]],"g":null}
